### What was retained from upstream

- The MIT license terms.
- The premise: a small, dependency-free set of CSS-driven interaction effects.
- The naming pattern for individual effects (bounce, pop, shake, swing…) informed the effect names used here.

### What was replaced

No upstream code is executed by the library. Nothing in `src/` is derived from upstream source. The upstream implementation was a single 180-line click handler that swapped a class on the element; this project replaces it wholesale:

| Concern | Upstream (micron v1.1.6) | This project |
|---|---|---|
| Trigger model | `click` only, delegated on `window` | Six trigger modes, bound per component |
| Interruption | None — animations stack or are lost | Explicit `latest` / `queue` / `lock` policy |
| Parameterisation | `data-micron-duration` (number only) | Duration, delay, easing (4 kinds), distance, scale, iterations |
| Easing | Four keywords | Cubic bezier solver, damped springs, steps, `linear()` serialisation |
| Reduced motion | Not handled at all (`grep -ri "prefers-reduced-motion"` → 0 hits) | Four strategies, enforced in CSS and JS, tested against a real OS media query |
| Accessibility | `aria-*` count: 0 | Roles, live regions, focus management, keyboard parity, tested |
| Component state | None — the DOM is the state | Explicit state machines with `getState()` |
| Export | None | CSS / WAAPI JS / standalone HTML / config JSON |
| Tests | `npm test` → `exit 1` ("no test specified") | 139 tests across 7 suites |

The `upstream/` directory is included as a licence and provenance artefact. It is not part of the build and is excluded from the release ZIP.

## Third-party references

- **easings.net** (Andrey Sitnik, Ivan Solovev) — the naming and canonical control points for the `cubic-bezier` presets in the demo's easing menu (Material standard, Material decelerate, Back out) follow the conventions popularised there. No code copied.
- **Damped harmonic oscillator** — the spring easing in `src/core/easing.js` is a direct implementation of the standard closed-form solution for a damped mass-spring system, which is textbook physics rather than borrowed code.

## Fonts and assets

No web fonts, icon fonts, or external assets are loaded. The check mark is drawn with CSS borders; the drag handle is a CSS `radial-gradient`. This is deliberate: the exported standalone HTML must run from `file://` with no network access, and the test suite asserts that no `href` or `src` points at an absolute URL.

